00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.480
For all of us on the Microcosmos team, Stentors&nbsp;
are the gift that is always giving. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:31.280
the first organism we did a deep dive&nbsp;
episode on because we wanted more people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:38.320
to get a chance to appreciate how magnificent&nbsp;
this very unappreciated unicellular organism is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:45.760
And now with our fancy new DIC microscope, we’ve&nbsp;
gotten the chance to appreciate their magnificence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:53.837
in new ways. It is amazing how changing bits&nbsp;
of light can give you a new perspective.&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.080
Scientists have been studying Stentors for well&nbsp;
over a century, and that’s given them plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:13.200
time to find new ways to learn about them. And&nbsp;
sometimes those methods are pretty normal. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:20.640
example, in 1995 there were reports that a lake&nbsp;
in Germany had turned black. And there was a clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:28.160
culprit: a large number of Stentor amethystinus.&nbsp;
And for one group of scientists, this “black-spot”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:34.240
bloom was an opportunity to put their plankton&nbsp;
nets to good use and collect some samples.&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.120
They gathered the Stentor amethystinus&nbsp;
particularly to study the purplish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.560
pigment amethystin that gives the species its&nbsp;
name, isolating the molecule so they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.560
determine its structure and compare it to other&nbsp;
pigments. The coloring is unique among stentors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.600
and it stands out particularly when you compare&nbsp;
it to the vivid blue of Stentor coeruleus.&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.640
Stentor coeruleus gets its color&nbsp;
from a pigment called stentorin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:06.560
which helps the stentor respond to light and&nbsp;
also serves a defensive function. And while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.240
Stentor coeruleus is a much-studied organism,&nbsp;
this pigment has turned up in strange places.&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.960
In the mid-2000s, people began observing that&nbsp;
the blue catfish of Eufaula Lake in Oklahoma were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:25.680
producing some strange eggs. Nothing was wrong&nbsp;
with them, but they were a reddish purple color.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.520
Roughly a quarter of the female catfish were&nbsp;
laying purple eggs instead of their normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:37.520
yellowish cream-colored eggs. And when scientists&nbsp;
investigated further, they found that the ovaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:45.840
of the female catfish had become purple as well.
All that color came from stentorin. The lake had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.880
likely experienced blooms of Stentor coeruleus,&nbsp;
which accumulated in the lake after the release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.240
of organic pollutants. As the Stentor’s&nbsp;
numbers increased, so did their consumption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.520
by the catfish. And that then drove to the&nbsp;
accumulation of stentorin inside the catfish’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:08.480
ovaries, affecting the color of the eggs.
Stentor coeruleus’s color makes it easy to spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.640
but other species can be more difficult to tell&nbsp;
apart. We’ve labeled this one Stentor roeseli,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.520
but to be completely honest, we’re&nbsp;
not sure if that’s exactly what it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:25.360
It might be a similar looking species called&nbsp;
Stentor magnus, but we can’t tell them apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.960
just by looking at them. If we had a Stentor&nbsp;
magnus and a Stentor roeseli side-by-side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.040
we could not tell you just by looking at&nbsp;
them whether they were the same organism.&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:43.520
There is, however, a weird and gruesome way to&nbsp;
solve that mystery. It’s called grafting. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:51.120
start, you take your two mystery stentors and you&nbsp;
cut them in half. Then you quickly take half of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:58.560
stentor number one and press it to half of stentor&nbsp;
number 2 at the exposed edge of both organisms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:05.840
Because they’re stentors and amazingly good at&nbsp;
regeneration, the two halves will fuse together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.800
like a “Frankenstein’s microbe.”
If your two original stentors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.680
are the same species, that fusion will&nbsp;
survive. If they’re two different species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:23.520
most likely it will die. Though intrepid stentor&nbsp;
stitches have found that the degree to which these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.880
different halves are incompatible varies with&nbsp;
different species and different combinations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.120
And we don’t know for certain which ones&nbsp;
will work, just that it’s a thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:39.280
maybe we should test out ourselves one day.
Underlying grafting is that remarkable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.960
regenerative ability of Stentors, which we’ve&nbsp;
described at length before. But to summarize:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.720
stentors are just really good at rebuilding&nbsp;
themselves when they need to. Maybe you’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.320
at this unicellular organism and thinking, “Well,&nbsp;
it’s just one cell, how complicated can it be?”&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.880
Pretty complicated, it turns out! So&nbsp;
complicated that we still don’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:09.600
know how this regeneration works. Scientists have&nbsp;
been trying to figure this out for over a century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.720
and the mystery remains unsolved though at&nbsp;
this point at least very well-documented.&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.320
And for more than a century, scientists have&nbsp;
been passing down techniques to cut stentors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.440
into pieces. There’s the obvious surgical&nbsp;
technique, using a needle to cut a stentor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.120
into pieces and then watch it reconstruct&nbsp;
several new versions of itself. But you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:38.000
also bathe stentors in a sugar solution, and the&nbsp;
organism will then shed its oral apparatus (which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:44.400
is its version of a mouth). It’s not clear why&nbsp;
this happens, but it allows scientists to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:51.040
the stentor rebuild that one portion of its body.
Scientists used this method to study how Stentor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:58.640
coeruleus resets the movement of its cilia after&nbsp;
regenerations. Cilia may look like little bits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.560
hair lining the organism, but they act more&nbsp;
like a coordinated set of limbs that help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:11.520
draw in food and move the stentor around. Lots&nbsp;
and lots of limbs that are moving together in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:17.840
complex dance that in turn creates patterns of&nbsp;
movement in the water surrounding the stentor.&nbsp;

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:24.000
And the scientists watched as the stentors&nbsp;
were able to rebuild this whole complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.080
system again. They found that it takes about 5&nbsp;
hours just to grow those oral cilia back to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:35.440
observable length, and then another two hours for&nbsp;
the cilia to start coordinating their movements.&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:43.040
So much work went into understanding the timeline&nbsp;
of this one narrow process in regeneration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:50.160
and there is still so much mechanical&nbsp;
mystery underlying it. Not to mention all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:56.320
the other feats of regenerative prowess that&nbsp;
stentors casually perform on a regular basis.&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:03.360
But science is always advancing. And with&nbsp;
all these mysteries to solve and more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.760
stentors will likely find&nbsp;
themselves at the center of many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:12.880
many other strange experiments&nbsp;
and observations to come.&nbsp;

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:20.240
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

